      "question_number": 11,
      "question_id": "mathematics_k-12_doc_04_mathematics_long_1",
      "question_type": "LONG",
      "stem_text": "The sequence $\\{a_n\\}$ satisfies $a_1 = 1$ and $5^{a_{n + 1} - a_n} - 1 = \\frac {1}{n +\n\\frac {2}{3}}$ for $n \\geq 1$. Find the least integer $k$ greater than $1$ for which $a_k$\nis an integer.",
      "options": null,
      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "We rewrite the given equation as \\[5^{a_{n+1} - a_n} = 1 + \\frac{1}{n +\\frac{2}{3}} =\n\\frac{3 n+5}{3 n+2}.\\]Then, we observe a telescoping product: \\[\\begin{aligned} 5^{a_n -\na_1} &= 5^{a_2 - a_1} \\cdot 5^{a_3-a_2} \\cdots 5^{a_n - a_{n-1}} \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{5} \\cdot\n\\frac{11}{8} \\cdots \\frac{3 n+2}{3 n-1} \\\\ &= \\frac{3 n+2}{5}. \\end{aligned}\\]Since $a_1 =\n1$, we have \\[5^{a_n} = 3 n+2\\]for all $n \\ge 1$. Thus, $a_k$ is an integer if and only if\n$3 k+2$ is a power of $5$. The next power of $5$ which is of the form $3 k+2$ is $5^3 =\n125$, which is $3(41) + 2$. Thus $k = \\boxed{41}$.",
      "gold_confidence": 1.0,
      "answer_explanation": "Students should show all steps in their mathematical solution.",
      "source": {
        "dataset": "gsm8k_math",
        "source_id": "17479"
      },
